draw the console's own bezel without redrawing it

Upstream's per-platform bezel is a full-frame image with a hole where the
picture goes, drawn over the game pass every frame. A full-frame blit is
345,600 pixels; this device has about a millisecond of headroom at 60 fps, so
every frame is not a price it can pay.

It does not have to. The band the bezel covers is the paper around the
picture, and the paper never changes: only the picture does. So the game pass
is cropped to the picture itself (`Game::crop`, set from `set_game_bezel`) and
the bezel is composited into the surround -- the panel-sized image painted when
the output rect moves and left alone otherwise, where the Advance SP shell
already lives. In play the bezel costs nothing per frame.

The canvas path still draws it per frame, as upstream does: a menu is up, the
canvas covers the panel, and the surround is not in view. Those frames belong
to a menu rather than to a game.

Two sizes that are the same upstream and differ here:

  - the art is made at the *picture's* frame, 720x480, not at the canvas,
    which is the 752x560 panel. The hole has to land on the picture, and
    `game_bezel_face` scaling it to the canvas put it 39px out on every side.
  - a still (`Draw::Shot`, the frame kept behind a menu) is drawn at the whole
    frame, paper and all, so it is not cropped.

`the_picture_fills_the_hole_in_the_art` pins the two together: the hole is
measured off bezel_gb.png and the crop is arithmetic, and nothing lines them
up at run time.

The bezel fills the panel rather than the picture's frame. The art is 720x480
-- the frame exactly -- which left 16px of bare panel down each side and 40
top and bottom, read on the handheld as a black border around the console
body. Scaling the art up would blur a texture, distort the lamps and
lettering, and carry the hole off the picture it exists to frame, so instead
the art is placed untouched at (16, 40) and the margin is filled with more of
the plastic already at each edge, mirrored outward.

Which plastic is found rather than assumed, since a user's own bezel will not
be built like these two: lines are taken from the edge while each stays within
1% of the one before it. Comparing against the outermost line instead measures
the art's own gentle shading -- the columns brighten 1.6% inwards -- and stops
the band in the middle of flat plastic; and a looser rule took the frame's
highlight row, 3.9% brighter than plastic, and mirrored it across the margin
as a visible line. Both of those were found by rendering a frame and reading
the row means out of it, which is also what the test does.

